<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10123"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 02, 2005 - 7:12 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Since those grids revealed his name and address, it might have been recognized by a few in however many thousands of people would have been trying to solve it: of course, it needed to be released to the public!!!! The police knew him, worked with him, and could not see it. They were too close. I find it unforgivable that the info was not released since the info the police kept to themselves made no sense to them apparently. I realize that hind sight is 20/20. That is why multiple source input is so vital. One small nuance caught by one person will make all the difference. I guess he played with dolls. I wonder if they found any anatomically correct ones in his collection.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
